## Frequently Asked Questions

### How can I feel more comfortable in social situations?

Start with manageable interactions, focus on what the other person is saying instead of monitoring yourself, and set small goals. Notice unhelpful assumptions about being judged and practice approaching situations gradually. If social fears significantly affect your life, a therapist can help you develop an individualized plan.

### Why do I feel awkward around people even when I want to make friends?

You may be worried about rejection, saying the wrong thing, or not knowing how to respond. Anxiety can also make you more focused on your own behavior than on the conversation. These experiences have different possible causes, so consider the situations in which they occur and how much they affect your life.

### Can therapy help me overcome my fear of social situations?

Therapy can help you understand social fears and practice new ways of responding to them. CBT, including gradual exposure to feared situations, is a well-studied approach for social anxiety disorder. Outcomes and treatment needs vary from person to person.

### What is the difference between social anxiety and introversion?

Introversion generally describes a preference for less stimulating or more solitary activities, while social anxiety involves fear or distress related to social evaluation. An introverted person may enjoy socializing but prefer smaller groups. Someone experiencing social anxiety may avoid interactions they actually want because they fear embarrassment or judgment. A person can be both introverted and socially anxious, but one does not automatically mean the other.

### How do I stop overthinking after a conversation?

Try setting a short period to reflect on what happened. Identify one thing that went well and one practical lesson, if there is one. Then redirect your attention to another activity. If you repeatedly replay conversations and criticize yourself, therapy may help you understand and change that pattern.

### Is it okay to take breaks from social situations?

Yes. Taking a short break to rest or regulate your emotions can be reasonable. However, if you regularly leave or avoid situations because of fear, consider whether avoidance is preventing you from doing things that matter to you. Gradual practice may help you find a more comfortable balance.

### Can online therapy help with social anxiety?

Psychotherapy can be delivered in person or virtually. NIMH notes that both in-person and telehealth psychotherapy can be effective for social anxiety disorder. Whether online therapy is a good fit depends on your needs, preferences, and access to appropriate care.

### How do I find a social anxiety therapist in Houston?

Look for a qualified mental health professional who has experience working with anxiety and can explain their treatment approach. Ask about experience with social fears, treatment goals, session format, fees, and insurance. Riaz Counseling offers individual therapy in the Houston area and online therapy throughout Texas; contact the practice to discuss your needs and current options.

## Conclusion

Feeling comfortable in social situations does not mean you will never experience nervousness, awkward pauses, or uncertainty about what to say.

It means learning that you can participate even when you feel unsure, recover from uncomfortable moments, and connect with people without expecting yourself to be perfect.

Start small. Focus on the person in front of you rather than trying to control every impression you make. Notice when fear encourages you to avoid situations that matter to you, and give yourself opportunities to practice at a manageable pace.

If social anxiety is affecting your relationships, work, education, or everyday life, therapy may help you understand the pattern and develop practical skills for moving forward.

**You do not have to become a different person to feel more comfortable around others. You can work toward greater social confidence while still being yourself.**

### Mental Health Disclaimer

This article is intended for educational purposes only and does not provide a diagnosis or replace individualized mental health care. Social discomfort can have different causes, and a qualified professional can help determine what type of support may be appropriate.

If you are experiencing severe distress or a mental health emergency, seek appropriate professional or emergency assistance.

